(1743375, 232527, 30584, '<td valign="top">ok, its not faked, but I completely see what you mean.. He is actually standing on our back porch (the toy is hanging from the edge of it.) Here is one of the outtakes that might give some more insight into how I did it. <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=237283" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30584/thumb/237283.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30584/thumb/237283.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30584/thumb/237283.jpg"/></a> <br/>and to tell the truth, I was hoping someone would have requested a DQ, would boost my ego for people to think I could do more than I actually know how to do, hehehe!<br/>~SiSi</td>', 'ok, its not faked, but I completely see what you mean.. He is actually standing on our back porch (the toy is hanging from the edge of it.) Here is one of the outtakes that might give some more insight into how I did it.  and to tell the truth, I was hoping someone would have requested a DQ, would boost my ego for people to think I could do more than I actually know how to do, hehehe!~SiSi', '2005-09-27 21:46:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(1743708, 232336, 40819, '<td valign="top">You ever notice something in your picture you didnt intend later on? Well I was surprised that nobody mentioned it while this was posted for the challenge; notice just to the left of center in the image towards the top the clouds? It looks like an eye looking back at you. Spooky, I swear I didnt do that.</td>', 'You ever notice something in your picture you didnt intend later on? Well I was surprised that nobody mentioned it while this was posted for the challenge; notice just to the left of center in the image towards the top the clouds? It looks like an eye looking back at you. Spooky, I swear I didnt do that.', '2005-09-27 23:49:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(1744127, 232749, 34171, '<td valign="top">Very cool Joey,<br/>You are making art with what ever is at hand and doing a damned good job of it in the process.</td>', 'Very cool Joey,You are making art with what ever is at hand and doing a damned good job of it in the process.', '2005-09-28 02:24:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(1744171, 232758, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>Very interesting image, very fitting for the conspiracy challenge.   I too like other voters would love to know if this is real or something you created.  If you did create this letter, awesome job obviously it looks real.  The photo itself is nice,  The background is distracting and takes away from the letter a little.  The red CLASSIFIED defiantly pops off of the page.  The red unfortunately draws some attention in the area of the upper right hand side where the white looks overdone.  Excellent job adding the pen to make sure you stayed within our guidelines.  The lighting is good, and I also the cropping you did not to include the whole paper.  Good job.</td>', '*Critique Club* Very interesting image, very fitting for the conspiracy challenge.   I too like other voters would love to know if this is real or something you created.  If you did create this letter, awesome job obviously it looks real.  The photo itself is nice,  The background is distracting and takes away from the letter a little.  The red CLASSIFIED defiantly pops off of the page.  The red unfortunately draws some attention in the area of the upper right hand side where the white looks overdone.  Excellent job adding the pen to make sure you stayed within our guidelines.  The lighting is good, and I also the cropping you did not to include the whole paper.  Good job.', '2005-09-28 02:34:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(1744185, 232697, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>Ok I will tell the truth at first I was like ok a tree where''s the conspiracy, but then I looked again and wow it just popped out at me!  Excellent idea! The photo itself is very interesting.  It seems like the whiteness on the branches is a little too strong but I could see if you did that to fit with the tower better.  This really is a great idea, and I bet if people spent the time to figure out what was before them they would have voted higher.  I would like to have seen the image cropped down just a little the sky looks overwhelming to me.  Maybe if the image was brought in a bit voters would have been able to see the tower and recognize it at first look.</td>', '*Critique Club* Ok I will tell the truth at first I was like ok a tree where''s the conspiracy, but then I looked again and wow it just popped out at me!  Excellent idea! The photo itself is very interesting.  It seems like the whiteness on the branches is a little too strong but I could see if you did that to fit with the tower better.  This really is a great idea, and I bet if people spent the time to figure out what was before them they would have voted higher.  I would like to have seen the image cropped down just a little the sky looks overwhelming to me.  Maybe if the image was brought in a bit voters would have been able to see the tower and recognize it at first look.', '2005-09-28 02:40:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(1744207, 232697, 1031, '<td valign="top">Thanks -- this was of several recent photos of mine which got over-lightened in post-processing as my monitor was getting far out of calibration. I''ll try to post some betteradjusted versions for comparison; also, I have some with the tree to the side -- I considered those, but went for the greater degree of camouflage.</td>', 'Thanks -- this was of several recent photos of mine which got over-lightened in post-processing as my monitor was getting far out of calibration. I''ll try to post some betteradjusted versions for comparison; also, I have some with the tree to the side -- I considered those, but went for the greater degree of camouflage.', '2005-09-28 02:48:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(1744216, 232742, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>Oh I am so lucky I got your photo, this was my favorite! What an awesome setup, I am sure for the time and effort you must be proud of such a  photo. The men are absolutely the best part, I love the time you spent.  I think a few voters probably would have voted higher if maybe you used a lighter colored lighter because it would have been more apparent.  That''s probably the biggest downfall is people missed what was in the jail.  I like that you used like a spot light to cast the shadows inside the jail, and even the shadows for the men.  The ship was a little distracting on the box, maybe it means something and I am just missing the point. Maybe this is a biast critique but I just love the work you did on this piece.  Excellent job, of course I think you should have scored higher.</td>', '*Critique Club* Oh I am so lucky I got your photo, this was my favorite! What an awesome setup, I am sure for the time and effort you must be proud of such a  photo. The men are absolutely the best part, I love the time you spent.  I think a few voters probably would have voted higher if maybe you used a lighter colored lighter because it would have been more apparent.  That''s probably the biggest downfall is people missed what was in the jail.  I like that you used like a spot light to cast the shadows inside the jail, and even the shadows for the men.  The ship was a little distracting on the box, maybe it means something and I am just missing the point. Maybe this is a biast critique but I just love the work you did on this piece.  Excellent job, of course I think you should have scored higher.', '2005-09-28 02:52:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(1744555, 232067, 30364, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club.<br/>It''s a good Conspiracy theme; the idea of unexplained barbed wire to prevent us from knowing what''s going on back there.  However, your image seems to me to suggest an old farm fence (which I guess it is).  The conspiracy kind of barbed wire ought to be shiny, new and a couple of metres high; that''s <i>really</i> sinister!  The image itself, its challenge theme shortcomings aside, is a nice one.  Good detail and composition.  I think some more contrast might have made it look even stronger.  That little fragment of wood hanging down in the centre adds nothing to the shot &amp; could profitably have been cloned out.  Better yet, broken off before you took the photograph!  All in all, a very good idea, but not perhaps executed for maximum impact.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club.It''s a good Conspiracy theme; the idea of unexplained barbed wire to prevent us from knowing what''s going on back there.  However, your image seems to me to suggest an old farm fence (which I guess it is).  The conspiracy kind of barbed wire ought to be shiny, new and a couple of metres high; that''s really sinister!  The image itself, its challenge theme shortcomings aside, is a nice one.  Good detail and composition.  I think some more contrast might have made it look even stronger.  That little fragment of wood hanging down in the centre adds nothing to the shot & could profitably have been cloned out.  Better yet, broken off before you took the photograph!  All in all, a very good idea, but not perhaps executed for maximum impact.', '2005-09-28 06:32:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(1744759, 232750, 41675, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :-)<br/> <br/>I think this was a good conceptual idea for the conspiracy challenge, though it takes a bit of thought for some to see the conspiracy, and some might not be able to understand until told.<br/> <br/>I like the off-centered composition of the pole itself. One thing I would have changed is to crop out the trees at the bottom as they are distracting. Visually, when I first look at it, the crossed wires make it look very busy to the eyes, but you have the nearly monochrome background to counteract that a little bit. After looking at the photo my eyes begin to recognize the negative space and shapes created by the wire patterns, and I like that part.<br/> <br/>With a photo that has what I call "breathing room" (or space), you might want to think about adding some sort of border to give it a finished feeling. I find choosing a color from within the photo always provides a nice compliment.<br/> <br/>Again, a good concept and nice take on the conspiracty challenge. I hope you find this information helpful.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :-) I think this was a good conceptual idea for the conspiracy challenge, though it takes a bit of thought for some to see the conspiracy, and some might not be able to understand until told. I like the off-centered composition of the pole itself. One thing I would have changed is to crop out the trees at the bottom as they are distracting. Visually, when I first look at it, the crossed wires make it look very busy to the eyes, but you have the nearly monochrome background to counteract that a little bit. After looking at the photo my eyes begin to recognize the negative space and shapes created by the wire patterns, and I like that part. With a photo that has what I call "breathing room" (or space), you might want to think about adding some sort of border to give it a finished feeling. I find choosing a color from within the photo always provides a nice compliment. Again, a good concept and nice take on the conspiracty challenge. I hope you find this information helpful.', '2005-09-28 09:26:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(1744783, 232641, 41675, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club.<br/> <br/>I think many, many viewers could relate to this conspiracy photo very easily.<br/> <br/>I like how you used the mirror, good angle. One thing I like to see in photos is having the subject slightly off-centered in the shot as it lends a bit more interest to the eyes. I find the pump hose a little distracting and I might have cropped in a bit tighter, say on the right (which would have placed the focus of your shot off center).<br/> <br/>Good lighting and the solid, vibrant colors work well also. It''s one of those photos where you just look, nod in agreement and wonder who those "conspiraotrs" are. In other words, you effectively and quickly communicated your message to the viewer. No tilting the head and trying to figure out what the photographer was trying to say. Nicely done.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club. I think many, many viewers could relate to this conspiracy photo very easily. I like how you used the mirror, good angle. One thing I like to see in photos is having the subject slightly off-centered in the shot as it lends a bit more interest to the eyes. I find the pump hose a little distracting and I might have cropped in a bit tighter, say on the right (which would have placed the focus of your shot off center). Good lighting and the solid, vibrant colors work well also. It''s one of those photos where you just look, nod in agreement and wonder who those "conspiraotrs" are. In other words, you effectively and quickly communicated your message to the viewer. No tilting the head and trying to figure out what the photographer was trying to say. Nicely done.', '2005-09-28 09:35:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(1745638, 232749, 13026, '<td valign="top">CONGRATULATIONS!  <br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-09-22 22:47:13.</i></td>', 'CONGRATULATIONS!  ', '2005-09-28 14:13:43.000000', '2006-09-22 23:47:13.000000', 0),
(1745652, 232744, 14986, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club.<br/> <br/>It really took me a minute to try and figure out what is going on in your photo.  It appears to be out of focus and this composition doesn''t really tell me a story about what''s going on.  I see flames, and I see people there - but that''s it.  <br/> <br/>My opinion is that you need to focus more on what will convey the message your photo intends to convey.   Ask yourself what a viewer (who was not present when you took the photo) would see in your image.  Is there a definable subject?  Is there a story or an appealing combination of color, lines, or light?   Then ask yourself about the technical aspects.  This one is out of focus and rather dark.  <br/> <br/>It is difficult to critique a photo that I feel needs a lot of work, so I do hope you find my ideas helpful.</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club. It really took me a minute to try and figure out what is going on in your photo.  It appears to be out of focus and this composition doesn''t really tell me a story about what''s going on.  I see flames, and I see people there - but that''s it.   My opinion is that you need to focus more on what will convey the message your photo intends to convey.   Ask yourself what a viewer (who was not present when you took the photo) would see in your image.  Is there a definable subject?  Is there a story or an appealing combination of color, lines, or light?   Then ask yourself about the technical aspects.  This one is out of focus and rather dark.   It is difficult to critique a photo that I feel needs a lot of work, so I do hope you find my ideas helpful.', '2005-09-28 14:18:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(1745669, 232759, 14986, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Great concept on this shot.  It gets the "point" across quite well (pun intended.)  Great choice of green for the liquid.<br/> <br/>How I think it could be stronger:  The lighting in the back seems really harsh to me and the creases in the material add to that effect.  Personally, I think the lighting needs a little work to lessen the glare of the nails, the harshness in back and perhaps light the hand just a bit -while still allowing light through the syringe.  <br/> <br/>I also think the harshness of the light adds to the slightly out of focus feeling I get looking at the photo.  I would have preferred a really crisp, clear focus on the syringe.  And lastly, I think it might have been interesting to have justa drop of the liquid hanging at the end of the needle. (yeah...how easy would that be? lol...but maybe worth a try.)  <br/> <br/>Overall, I really like the shot and just think it would benefit from a more polished and finished look in the details.  <br/> <br/>Hope this is helpful!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Great concept on this shot.  It gets the "point" across quite well (pun intended.)  Great choice of green for the liquid. How I think it could be stronger:  The lighting in the back seems really harsh to me and the creases in the material add to that effect.  Personally, I think the lighting needs a little work to lessen the glare of the nails, the harshness in back and perhaps light the hand just a bit -while still allowing light through the syringe.   I also think the harshness of the light adds to the slightly out of focus feeling I get looking at the photo.  I would have preferred a really crisp, clear focus on the syringe.  And lastly, I think it might have been interesting to have justa drop of the liquid hanging at the end of the needle. (yeah...how easy would that be? lol...but maybe worth a try.)   Overall, I really like the shot and just think it would benefit from a more polished and finished look in the details.   Hope this is helpful!', '2005-09-28 14:28:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(1745682, 232056, 26255, '<td valign="top"><b>**Critique Club**</b> <br/> <br/><b><u>Initial Impression:</u></b>  Funny image that falls a little flat.<br/> <br/><b><u>Detailed analysis:</u></b> Although this image does meet the challenge very well IMHO, there are a couple of things that could help it do better.  As the others stated, the lighting is a little flat.  It appears as though it was raining at the time you took this photo which could account for the flat lighting (overcast skies).  I think you could probably adjust the hue/saturation and contrast to help add more interest.  The position of the crow in relation to the pelican on the right is rather unfortunate.  It almost appears as if there is something wrong with the pelicans head.  The crow is not very obvious.  A slightly different angle would probably eliminate this particular problem and help better differentiate the three birds.  I love the title and your explanation of the situation in the comments.<br/> <br/><b><u>Overall Impression:</u></b>  Pretty good shot that needs some minor tweaking.  A little more patience in the composition and some post-processing would help this a lot.</td>', '**Critique Club**  Initial Impression:  Funny image that falls a little flat. Detailed analysis: Although this image does meet the challenge very well IMHO, there are a couple of things that could help it do better.  As the others stated, the lighting is a little flat.  It appears as though it was raining at the time you took this photo which could account for the flat lighting (overcast skies).  I think you could probably adjust the hue/saturation and contrast to help add more interest.  The position of the crow in relation to the pelican on the right is rather unfortunate.  It almost appears as if there is something wrong with the pelicans head.  The crow is not very obvious.  A slightly different angle would probably eliminate this particular problem and help better differentiate the three birds.  I love the title and your explanation of the situation in the comments. Overall Impression:  Pretty good shot that needs some minor tweaking.  A little more patience in the composition and some post-processing would help this a lot.', '2005-09-28 14:35:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(1745729, 232671, 34192, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Beautiful image of the moon.  I would agree that there is some apparent color noise on the moon.  I would suggest desaturation to get rid of it - the image is almost black and white anyway.<br/>The composition works - its at rule of thirds which many people prefer.  The moon itself is a little soft but I would think noise and resizing did most of that.  <br/>I think the main reason this did not score well in the challenge is that it ''kind of'' fit the challenge but it didn''t do it well enough for the voters.<br/> <br/>Please feel free to PM with any questions or comments!<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Beautiful image of the moon.  I would agree that there is some apparent color noise on the moon.  I would suggest desaturation to get rid of it - the image is almost black and white anyway.The composition works - its at rule of thirds which many people prefer.  The moon itself is a little soft but I would think noise and resizing did most of that.  I think the main reason this did not score well in the challenge is that it ''kind of'' fit the challenge but it didn''t do it well enough for the voters. Please feel free to PM with any questions or comments!', '2005-09-28 14:56:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(1745734, 232749, 26255, '<td valign="top"><b>**Critique Club**</b> <br/> <br/><b><u>Initial Impression:</u></b> I''m not qualified to critique this shot.<br/> <br/><b><u>Detailed analysis:</u></b> Joey, you have to know this is a spectacular shot and a well deserved ribbon.  Your talent stretches well above and beyond my own and I''m twice your age!  Your comments just further prove your creativity and ability to make the most of a situation.<br/> <br/>Since this is supposed to be a critique, I''ll give it my best shot... here goes.<br/> <br/>The composition is spot on and I love the dramatic lighting.  Your light position helps draw attention to the fog and casts very strong shadows on your profile to give a very dark and mysterious feel.  There were comments on the patch of green grass, but personally I like it.  It adds to the MIB realism... On a surreal, mysterious case in a very real environment.  The polka dots on the tie don''t exactly fit, but it''s a detail that becomes lost in the otherwise flawless execution.<br/> <br/><b><u>Overall Impression:</u></b> Excellent shot, well thought out and executed.  Well deserved ribbon!<br/> <br/>I normally end my critiques here, but I wanted to add a side note in your case.  When I first became more active in DPC, there was much talk about this young prodigy with a very "grunge" look.  After a while your "style" became very obvious and I also noticed that many were trying to mimic the style.  It''s nice to see that lately you seem to have evolved to shoot images that are "outside" of your proverbial box.  This image, although dark, contains none of the trademark "Joey grunge" and your destinations ribbon winner is incredibly colorful.  I''m not sure if this is the beginning of some type of transition in your life, but it is very refreshing to see you evolve and develop as a very talented artist.  As you continue to develop and grow as a photographer and videographer, I wish you great success.<br/> <br/>-Lee</td>', '**Critique Club**  Initial Impression: I''m not qualified to critique this shot. Detailed analysis: Joey, you have to know this is a spectacular shot and a well deserved ribbon.  Your talent stretches well above and beyond my own and I''m twice your age!  Your comments just further prove your creativity and ability to make the most of a situation. Since this is supposed to be a critique, I''ll give it my best shot... here goes. The composition is spot on and I love the dramatic lighting.  Your light position helps draw attention to the fog and casts very strong shadows on your profile to give a very dark and mysterious feel.  There were comments on the patch of green grass, but personally I like it.  It adds to the MIB realism... On a surreal, mysterious case in a very real environment.  The polka dots on the tie don''t exactly fit, but it''s a detail that becomes lost in the otherwise flawless execution. Overall Impression: Excellent shot, well thought out and executed.  Well deserved ribbon! I normally end my critiques here, but I wanted to add a side note in your case.  When I first became more active in DPC, there was much talk about this young prodigy with a very "grunge" look.  After a while your "style" became very obvious and I also noticed that many were trying to mimic the style.  It''s nice to see that lately you seem to have evolved to shoot images that are "outside" of your proverbial box.  This image, although dark, contains none of the trademark "Joey grunge" and your destinations ribbon winner is incredibly colorful.  I''m not sure if this is the beginning of some type of transition in your life, but it is very refreshing to see you evolve and develop as a very talented artist.  As you continue to develop and grow as a photographer and videographer, I wish you great success. -Lee', '2005-09-28 14:58:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(1745752, 232798, 34192, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Since you did not include any comments, this critique will be brief.<br/> <br/>There are many things with which to judge an image:  Composition, color/tonal balance, focus, POV, DOF, overall impression<br/>Your composition here works well.  In the ''best world, you would be slightly to your right and maybe your foot would hide less of your face, but for an action shot this works really well.<br/>I like the use of B&amp;W.  I think it is effective for this image.  I think you cover the range of tones well - you have blacks and whites.<br/>The image is in focus, the DOV works for the image and the POV is excellent - very creative.<br/>My overall impression is that this is a well executed image.<br/>With regard to the challenge:  At DPC there is a tendency for voters to dislike noise and less than tack sharp images.  If you did not have both of those, the image would definitely have scored higher.  I am in no way saying that is the best thing for the image; I am saying that is what voters prefer.  I also think the title does not quite fit:  the man is kicking but the title refers to psychic spies.  I can''t see a relation.<br/>If you have any questions or comments, please do not hesitate to PM me.<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Since you did not include any comments, this critique will be brief. There are many things with which to judge an image:  Composition, color/tonal balance, focus, POV, DOF, overall impressionYour composition here works well.  In the ''best world, you would be slightly to your right and maybe your foot would hide less of your face, but for an action shot this works really well.I like the use of B&W.  I think it is effective for this image.  I think you cover the range of tones well - you have blacks and whites.The image is in focus, the DOV works for the image and the POV is excellent - very creative.My overall impression is that this is a well executed image.With regard to the challenge:  At DPC there is a tendency for voters to dislike noise and less than tack sharp images.  If you did not have both of those, the image would definitely have scored higher.  I am in no way saying that is the best thing for the image; I am saying that is what voters prefer.  I also think the title does not quite fit:  the man is kicking but the title refers to psychic spies.  I can''t see a relation.If you have any questions or comments, please do not hesitate to PM me. ', '2005-09-28 15:07:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(1746365, 232742, 41918, '<td valign="top">Thanks to all for the advice and nice comments. For those that are wondering, the whole picture was made from a box of (ship) matches, some superglue, a lighter and the flashlight on my mobile phone.<br/> <br/>I started this at 10pm and submitted the photo at 3am GMT. The model took about 3 hours. The biggest trouble was getting the superglue to attach the matchsticks together!<br/> <br/>Oh yeah. And I am SO chuffed with the 21st place! Thanks.<br/> <br/> <br/> <br/> <br/>Edit:typo<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2005-09-28 18:29:11.</i></td>', 'Thanks to all for the advice and nice comments. For those that are wondering, the whole picture was made from a box of (ship) matches, some superglue, a lighter and the flashlight on my mobile phone. I started this at 10pm and submitted the photo at 3am GMT. The model took about 3 hours. The biggest trouble was getting the superglue to attach the matchsticks together! Oh yeah. And I am SO chuffed with the 21st place! Thanks.    Edit:typo', '2005-09-28 19:27:17.000000', '2005-09-28 19:29:11.000000', 0),
(1746601, 232328, 37730, '<td valign="top">*<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2007-10-24 15:44:07.</i></td>', '*', '2005-09-28 20:53:03.000000', '2007-10-24 16:44:07.000000', 0),
(1746685, 232665, 37544, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>I think that this picture is VERY CREATIVE and you are just a genius. When I first saw it I was just in shock. At first glance it is kind of disturbing. The blue hue really adds to the frozen look. The topic you chose for the theme didn''t really make sense to me. A frozen clone army of children from Canada? I don''t get it. Overall I like the picture. I wouldn''t change anything. This picture creeps me out.<br/> <br/>Mandy</td>', '*Critique Club* I think that this picture is VERY CREATIVE and you are just a genius. When I first saw it I was just in shock. At first glance it is kind of disturbing. The blue hue really adds to the frozen look. The topic you chose for the theme didn''t really make sense to me. A frozen clone army of children from Canada? I don''t get it. Overall I like the picture. I wouldn''t change anything. This picture creeps me out. Mandy', '2005-09-28 21:15:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(1746712, 232030, 19978, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/>by strangeghost<br/> <br/><b>COMPOSITION</b> <br/>Wow. Quite a strong photo with a great concept to grab DPCers'' attention! I love the "in your face" centeredness because the photo is all about her being in your face. I might have considered a slightly closer crop, but I''m sure you considered that too, and went with your actual crop for good reason. The costume, the face paint and facial expression, the pose, open mouth rage, and angry sky background all make for a very compelling and strong composition. Good job.<br/> <br/><b>TECHNIQUE</b> <br/>Very well lit with strong, sharp contrasty colors. Excellent job on the dodging and burning of the sky (is it a sky?). Technically a very strong shot.<br/> <br/><b>OVERALL IMPACT</b> <br/>This is quite a high impact shot, IMO. So why didn''t it do better (though 5.6 is a respectable score)? Like most people shots, it might have scored better if you had gotten tighter on the face, but this would clearly have made it very difficult to retain the other elements. I like it though, and hope you''re happy with your work. <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club by strangeghost COMPOSITION Wow. Quite a strong photo with a great concept to grab DPCers'' attention! I love the "in your face" centeredness because the photo is all about her being in your face. I might have considered a slightly closer crop, but I''m sure you considered that too, and went with your actual crop for good reason. The costume, the face paint and facial expression, the pose, open mouth rage, and angry sky background all make for a very compelling and strong composition. Good job. TECHNIQUE Very well lit with strong, sharp contrasty colors. Excellent job on the dodging and burning of the sky (is it a sky?). Technically a very strong shot. OVERALL IMPACT This is quite a high impact shot, IMO. So why didn''t it do better (though 5.6 is a respectable score)? Like most people shots, it might have scored better if you had gotten tighter on the face, but this would clearly have made it very difficult to retain the other elements. I like it though, and hope you''re happy with your work. ', '2005-09-28 21:29:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(1746714, 232703, 37544, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>I love this picture. The pose is great. The colors and texture of the jacket really POP out. The shadows are very nice, they add some mood to the photo. What I don''t like the color of the skin. It is so red. Did it get this way because you upped the saturation of color? My mother would say that you had a fever because your ears are so red. LOL I would have fixed the skin color by selective desat or added a 50% transparent sepia layer. I would also love to see this in black and white. <br/> <br/>Mandy</td>', '*Critique Club* I love this picture. The pose is great. The colors and texture of the jacket really POP out. The shadows are very nice, they add some mood to the photo. What I don''t like the color of the skin. It is so red. Did it get this way because you upped the saturation of color? My mother would say that you had a fever because your ears are so red. LOL I would have fixed the skin color by selective desat or added a 50% transparent sepia layer. I would also love to see this in black and white.  Mandy', '2005-09-28 21:29:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(1746828, 232684, 9111, '<td valign="top">**Critique Club**<br/>How do you critique a photo that is this good??  <br/>The reflection of the moon in the dixie cup holder adds to the mystery of the photo.  I think if you had removed the thing in the upper right hand corner it may have added points to this photo. As it is, thats the only thing I can see that might improve this photo. The moon is crisp and clear which is important.<br/>Keep up the great work :) <br/></td>', '**Critique Club**How do you critique a photo that is this good??  The reflection of the moon in the dixie cup holder adds to the mystery of the photo.  I think if you had removed the thing in the upper right hand corner it may have added points to this photo. As it is, thats the only thing I can see that might improve this photo. The moon is crisp and clear which is important.Keep up the great work :) ', '2005-09-28 22:18:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(1746907, 232749, 25232, '<td valign="top">You''re going places Joey.</td>', 'You''re going places Joey.', '2005-09-28 22:39:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(1747184, 232408, 41675, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the critique club.<br/> <br/>I liked the concept of your entry for the challenges. People have long believed that UFO''s are indeed government conspiracies, etc.<br/> <br/>I like the lighting - the orange glowing type effect. The thing that made this less "realistic" were the two semi-circular shapes on the "spaceship" itself, and the rim.<br/> <br/>Simplicity in composition works well, and I like the solid black background. It keeps the viewers eyes on your subject matter. I do find the white border just a bit distracting as there is no other "true" white in your photo. Sometimes I find choosing a color from the photo itself tends to lessen any such distraction.<br/> <br/>Overall though, good entry in the challenge. It shows a cleverness and creativity. Keep up the good work.</td>', 'Greetings from the critique club. I liked the concept of your entry for the challenges. People have long believed that UFO''s are indeed government conspiracies, etc. I like the lighting - the orange glowing type effect. The thing that made this less "realistic" were the two semi-circular shapes on the "spaceship" itself, and the rim. Simplicity in composition works well, and I like the solid black background. It keeps the viewers eyes on your subject matter. I do find the white border just a bit distracting as there is no other "true" white in your photo. Sometimes I find choosing a color from the photo itself tends to lessen any such distraction. Overall though, good entry in the challenge. It shows a cleverness and creativity. Keep up the good work.', '2005-09-29 00:24:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(1747206, 232642, 41675, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club.<br/> <br/>For me, if you were to just show me this photo and say, guess what this represents, I would not think of the word conspiracy. In that regards, it''s difficult to know what you were thinking when you took this. If perhaps there was a faded moon in the background, it would have aided in the overall conspiracy effect.<br/> <br/>I do like the angle and composition you choose. Some might not like the softness, but I think it works okay here. The faded blue adds to that softness, whereas a newer flag wouldn''t have that same feel.<br/> <br/>The lighting is also good and the solid black background keeps my attention and focus on your subject matter.<br/> <br/>I look forward to seeing more of your work.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club. For me, if you were to just show me this photo and say, guess what this represents, I would not think of the word conspiracy. In that regards, it''s difficult to know what you were thinking when you took this. If perhaps there was a faded moon in the background, it would have aided in the overall conspiracy effect. I do like the angle and composition you choose. Some might not like the softness, but I think it works okay here. The faded blue adds to that softness, whereas a newer flag wouldn''t have that same feel. The lighting is also good and the solid black background keeps my attention and focus on your subject matter. I look forward to seeing more of your work.', '2005-09-29 00:36:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(1747255, 232336, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club* <br/> <br/>Wow New Jersey, I don''t think any of the voters guessed (I know I didn''t).  This is an amazing shot.  I think the coloring is my favorite. The brown just demands attention, how often do you see a backdrop that looks like this.  The is a wonderful contrast between the foreground and the background really bringing the image to life.  I really do not see anything that could be improved on the photo. I did read your post about the eye and that is freaky.  I think your placement was fairly decent, I think voters were probably just looking for crazy conspiracy ideas, like aliens and such.  Excellent job!</td>', '*Critique Club*  Wow New Jersey, I don''t think any of the voters guessed (I know I didn''t).  This is an amazing shot.  I think the coloring is my favorite. The brown just demands attention, how often do you see a backdrop that looks like this.  The is a wonderful contrast between the foreground and the background really bringing the image to life.  I really do not see anything that could be improved on the photo. I did read your post about the eye and that is freaky.  I think your placement was fairly decent, I think voters were probably just looking for crazy conspiracy ideas, like aliens and such.  Excellent job!', '2005-09-29 00:57:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(1747279, 232284, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club* <br/> <br/>Ok I saw this during voting and was just waiting to find out what A. F. &amp; G. meant, but I guess it will always be unexplained... must be a conspiracy.  This a great photo and a very well deserved Top 20.  I think the best thing about the image is the DOF.  I love the shadowing used in the foreground, and the blur in the background. I think the pink defiantly define the background person as a girl which I think was key in this image.  Ok looking at the image really makes me wonder are these real people? There sure is a plastic look to them especially the guy on the left.  This is just a great image, I really don''t see any area for improvement.  I know there has been a comment or too about more focus, but I think it would take away from the subject. Great Job!</td>', '*Critique Club*  Ok I saw this during voting and was just waiting to find out what A. F. & G. meant, but I guess it will always be unexplained... must be a conspiracy.  This a great photo and a very well deserved Top 20.  I think the best thing about the image is the DOF.  I love the shadowing used in the foreground, and the blur in the background. I think the pink defiantly define the background person as a girl which I think was key in this image.  Ok looking at the image really makes me wonder are these real people? There sure is a plastic look to them especially the guy on the left.  This is just a great image, I really don''t see any area for improvement.  I know there has been a comment or too about more focus, but I think it would take away from the subject. Great Job!', '2005-09-29 01:09:44.000000', NULL, 0),
